<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">The
Companies Act contains significant changes to Indian company law, including in relation to the issuance of capital by companies, related
party transactions, corporate governance, audit matters, shareholder class actions and restrictions on the number of layers of subsidiaries
and corporate social responsibility spending.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><b>Litigation</b></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">From
time to time, we may become involved in legal proceedings or be subject to claims arising in the ordinary course of our business and
the results of litigation and claims cannot be predicted with certainty.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">Except
for the Legal proceedings and tax proceedings described below, there are no other governmental, legal or arbitration proceedings (including
any such proceedings which are pending or threatened, of which we are aware) that we believe could reasonably be expected to have a material
adverse effect on our results of operations or financial position.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><b>Legal
proceedings</b></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">TSI
Yatra Private Limited (&#8220;TSI Yatra&#8221;), the indirect subsidiary of Yatra India was served a demand notice under the Insolvency
and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 (&#8220;IBC&#8221;) by Ezeego One Travel and Tours Limited, through its Resolution Professional (&#8220;Ezeego&#8221;)
demanding payment of unpaid operational debt of INR 148.69 million under the Bank Confirmation Agreement. TSI Yatra disputed the amount
by stating that its books of accounts only reflect an outstanding amount of INR 40.32 million, which has not yet been paid due to the
pending reconciliation of these accounts. In accordance with the provisions of the IBC, Ezeego filed a Company Petition seeking initiation
of Corporate Insolvency Resolution Process (&#8220;CIRP&#8221;) against TSI Yatra before the National Company Law Tribunal, New Delhi
(&#8220;NCLT&#8221;), for a default amounting to INR 219.77 million (including interest till date of filing). TSI Yatra had filed its
response disputing the claims. The Hon&#8217;ble NCLT, by vide its order dated October 15, 2024 (&#8220;Impugned Order&#8221;), had admitted
the Company Petition filed by Ezeego. TSI Yatra has filed an appeal against the said Impugned Order before the National Company Law Appellate
Tribunal, New Delhi (&#8220;NCLAT&#8221;). The Hon&#8217;ble NCLAT, vide its order dated October 18, 2024, stayed the operation of the
Impugned Order and directed TSI Yatra to deposit the admitted amount i.e. INR 40.32 million in favour of Registrar, NCLAT.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><b><i></i></b></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt">&#160;</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0; text-align: justify">The parties entered into a settlement agreement dated
November 19, 2025, under which TSI paid Ezeego INR 50.00 million as settlement amount and INR 0.63 million/- towards CIRP costs by demand
drafts, in full and final settlement of the dispute before the NCLAT, subject to Ezeego withdrawing the IBC proceedings against TSI (&#8220;Settlement&#8221;).
The NCLAT disposed of the appeal by directing: (i) refund of INR 40.32 million deposited under the Stay Order to TSI; (ii) the Interim
Resolution Professional (&#8220;IRP&#8221;) to file an application under Section 12A of the IBC read with Regulation 30A of the IBBI (Insolvency
Resolution Process for Corporate Persons) Regulations, 2016 (&#8220;Withdrawal Application&#8221;) for withdrawal of the CIRP; and (iii)
that further CIRP steps be taken only after the NCLT decides the Withdrawal Application. Accordingly, the IRP filed the Withdrawal Application
before the NCLT, seeking withdrawal of the CIRP and Company Petition. By order dated 15.12.2025, the NCLT allowed the application and
permitted withdrawal of the Company Petition and CIRP.</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><b><i>Tax
Matters Relating to Yatra India</i></b></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Assessment
Year 2020-21</span></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">In
February 2024, the Income Tax department issued a notice for proceeding u/s 201 TDS matters. The issue was primarily on account of
disallowance claimed under section 40(a)(ia) of income tax act. We submitted the requisite details and Order u/s 201 for AY 2020-21
dated 18.3.2026 and nil order is passed.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Assessment Year
2021-24</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in">&#160;</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify">In February 2024, the Income
Tax department issued a notice for proceeding u/s 201 this is for AY 2021-24 for TDS matters. Subsequently, the details were submitted
with the department and no further communication from the department till now. The matter is still ongoing.</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Assessment
Year 2022-23</span></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">In
November 2023, the Income Tax Department initiated Transfer Pricing proceedings under Section 92CA of the
Income-tax Act, 1961. The Company submitted the requisite documentation in response to the notices on November 21, 2023, December 26, 2023,
and May 21, 2024. On
January 13, 2025, the Company received an order under Section 92CA(3) in its favor. A closure order was issued on
the same date, reflecting a NIL tax demand. Accordingly,
the Transfer Pricing proceedings stand concluded. Separately, the Department initiated assessment proceedings by issuing a notice
under Section 142(1) on September 6, 2023. During the relevant fiscal year, the Company reimbursed INR 187.72 million to its
Associated Enterprise (AE). In this context, the Assessing Officer (AO) examined the allowability of these expenses under Section
37(1) and the applicability and compliance of tax deduction at source (TDS) provisions under Section 40(a)(ia). The Company
submitted its response on October 4, 2023. A subsequent notice under the same section was issued on November 11, 2024, to which the
Company responded on December 17, 2024. On March 25, 2025, the authorities issued an assessment order under Section 143(3),
disallowing the aforementioned reimbursement expense of INR 187.72 million. However, the corresponding demand notice issued under
Section 156 reflected a NIL demand. The Company filed an appeal against the disallowance before the Commissioner of Income Tax
(Appeals) [CIT(A)] on April 21, 2025. A response to the CIT(A) was filed on June 8, 2026, and the order is currently awaited. The
assessment matter remains pending before the CIT(A).</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in">&#160;</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"></p>

