<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><b><i>Tax
Matters Relating to Yatra TG Stays Private Limited (formerly known as D. V. Travels Guru Pvt. Ltd)</i></b></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Assessment
Year 2017-18</span></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">In
September 2019, the Company received a notice from the Indian Income Tax authorities for scrutiny assessment for Assessment Year
2017-18. An assessment order under Section 143(3) was passed by the Department on December 25, 2019, wherein advertisement and sales
promotion expenses and employees&#8217; contribution to the Employees&#8217; State Insurance Corporation (&#8220;ESIC&#8221;)
amounting to INR 20 million were disallowed. The Company filed an appeal before the Commissioner of Income Tax (Appeals) under
Section 246A against the said order on January 22, 2020. In January 2021, the Company applied under the Direct Tax Vivad se Vishwas
Scheme (&#8220;VSVS&#8221;) to settle the tax dispute with the Department. The Company received an order for full and final
settlement of the tax arrears under the relevant provisions of the Direct Tax VSVS Act, 2020 (3 of 2020) and the Direct Tax VSVS
Rules, 2020, dated November 24, 2021. Accordingly, this matter stands concluded.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in">Separately, for the same Assessment
Year, the Company received a demand notice of INR 0.86 million, wherein the Department stated that the refund had been received twice.
The Company filed a rectification application under Section 154, as factually the refund was received only once and not twice as stated
by the Assessing Officer (AO).</p>
<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in">The rectification application is currently pending disposal. Based on the facts of the case, management believes that the Company has
a strong case on merits and a favorable outcome is likely.</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify">&#160;</p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Assessment
Year 2015-16</span></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">The
assessment unit completed the proceedings on best judgement basis under section 144 of the Act and passed order dt. May 23, 2023, under
section 147 and 144 of the Act, at an assessed at loss of Rs. 211.96m making an addition of excess claim of depreciation on Computer
software amounting to Rs. 6.49m. Penalty proceedings were initiated by the Faceless Assessing officer for furnishing inaccurate particulars
of its income. Subsequently, show cause notices were issued by Ld. AO for imposing penalty under section 271(1)(c) of the Act and thereafter,
order u/s 271(1)(c) was passed by alleging that the Company has furnished inaccurate particulars of income to the extent of Rs.6.49m.
Accordingly, penalty amounting to INR 1.95m being 100 percent of tax evaded is levied u/s.271(1)(c) of the Act. The company filed an
appeal against the impugned order and detailed reply was filed with Indian Tax Authorities (CIT(A)) on 20 November 2024 the matter is
yet to be disposed by CIT(A).</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt"><span style="text-decoration: underline">Service
Tax Show Cause and Demand Notice-November 2005 to October 2006</span></span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 10pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: -10pt"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">In
April 2011, we received a notice from the service tax authorities based on investigation carried out by the DGCEI for the period November
2005 to October 2006 in respect of admissibility of input credit. In November 2012, we filed a reply with the Commissioner of Service
Tax. The value of demand for the show cause notice above was approximately INR 3.7 million (excluding interest and penalties if finally
determined to be payable). A personal hearing on the matter was held on July 27, 2016, and we reiterated the submissions on the ground
of appeal and also made additional written submissions. In August 2016, we received orders wherein the demand was confirmed by the Commissioner
(Appeals). We filed an appeal against the order with the Customs, Excise and Service Tax Appellate Tribunal (CESTAT) in November 2016.
The matter was listed before CESTAT, Mumbai on June 29, 2017. Tribunal after hearing the submissions of the parties was pleased to allow
the Appeal by way of remand to adjudicating authority. The Bench directed the Authority to duly verify the receipt of services by the
Appellant and allow credit on services, if found to have been received by the Company. The Adjudicating Authority was directed to complete
the process of verification by December 31, 2017, and pass appropriate order. We by letter dated November 17, 2017, asked Asst. Commissioner
for any information/details are required and extended our support to complete verification process in response of which we have received
the letter from department dated December 22, 2017 that Asst Commissioner had no jurisdiction to adjudicate the said Order and hence
has requested to approach the adjudicating authority as per Para 5 of circular 1049/37/2016-CX under F.No.267/40/2016-CX by virtue of
which any cases remanded back for novo jurisdiction can be decided by the authority of the rank which passed the remanded order. On January
16, 2018, we filed 1 letter with the Additional Commissioner CGST requesting to complete the inquiry.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">On
December 24, 2018, we received the notice from the Superintendent CGST scheduling a personal hearing with the Joint Commissioner CGST
on January 8, 2019. During the hearing, the officer requested a list of vendors along with their addresses for verification purposes.
We provided the requested information on January 15, 2019. As of now, there has been no further communication from the Joint Commissioner&#8217;s
office.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0.25in"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">Matter
was therefore remanded back by CESTAT to jurisdictional officers and is pending adjudication.</span></p>

<p style="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 0; text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, Times, Serif; font-size: 10pt">&#160;</span></p>


